Home-made trial image (2026-11-20) - NOT a vendor build.
  mcb_1118prog_pinks_no13_endalign.bin
"Plan D": plan C (FW_261119/mcb_1118prog_seq0923_pinks_no13.bin) with each
colour's trailing rest moved to just after the shake, so all colours finish
switching together. Made here with tools/fw_waveform.py retime --end-align.
Program: still the vendor's FW_261118 trial program, byte for byte.
Table: the same steps as plan C in every colour (header total 662). The
drive pulses and their order are unchanged; only WHEN the rest happens.
Each colour's trailing whole steps of pure level-0 rest now sit right after
its shake, so it waits first and then drives, finishing at tick ~662
(10 ms ticks) instead of anywhere from 454 (0x0C) to 661 (0x0D).
The shake ends at tick 354 in 12 colours. Yellow (1) has a shake 10 ticks
longer (ends at 364) and gets its rest there. Green (5), turquoise (6) and
almond (7) shake until 426 but have no trailing rest, so they are untouched,
like red (3), brown (13), dark brown (14) and smoky blue (15). A rest inside
a colour's last driving step cannot be moved and stays where it is.
Unknown: whether a longer rest between shake and colour drive (up to ~2 s
for 0x0C, 1.5 s for light pink) changes the resulting colours - compare
the colours against plan C by eye.
A second unknown: eight colours (0x00, 0x01, 0x02, 0x04, 0x08, 0x0A, 0x0B,
0x0C) now drive until the very last tick with no level-0 hold after it.
FW_260923 never does that (FW_260903 did, in seven colours). If colours
look wrong right at the end, or edges / ghosting appear, suspect this as
well as the longer rest before the colour drive.
Unproven on hardware: try it on a spare board first.
Reproduce (from the repo root; it refuses while the .bin exists - delete
it first or add --force):
  python tools/fw_waveform.py retime --base FW/archive/FW_261119/mcb_1118prog_seq0923_pinks_no13.bin --end-align --out FW/archive/FW_261119D/mcb_1118prog_pinks_no13_endalign.bin
